# A language server in a child process

Most languages already have a language server: a program that answers an editor's questions about the language over the Language Server Protocol, LSP for short. Rust has rust-analyzer, Python has Pyright, Go has gopls, and TypeScript 7 has `tsc --lsp`. Loom does not rebuild those servers. A language's package starts its server as a child process and asks it about the composed code, the same way an editor would ask about a file on disk.

`LanguageServerProcess` is that bridge, and nothing in it knows which language it serves. It starts a server, keeps the files it is given open in the server, and sends the server requests. TypeScript 7 is the first package to use it, and the package for any other language reuses it unchanged.

## What the bridge offers

A language server answers only about files it knows, and a composed file exists nowhere on disk, or exists there as an older tangle. So the bridge sends each file's text to the server as an open document, which the server reads in place of the disk. `keepOpen` takes the whole set of files that should be open, each an `OpenFile` with its path and text. It opens the files new to the set, sends the new text of the files that changed, and closes the files no longer in it. Every call names the full set, so the caller never tracks what the server has already seen.

`request` sends one request and resolves with the server's answer. `alive` says whether the server is still running, so its owner can decide whether to start another. `uptime` says how long the server ran, until now or until it stopped, so its owner can tell a server that ran for a while from one that failed as soon as it started. `dispose` asks the server to shut down and stops the process.

=>

export interface OpenFile {
  readonly path: string
  readonly text: string
}

export interface LanguageServerProcess {
  readonly keepOpen: (files: ReadonlyArray<OpenFile>) => void
  readonly request: <A>(method: string, params: unknown) => Promise<A>
  readonly alive: () => boolean
  readonly uptime: () => number
  readonly dispose: () => void
}

~

A request fails with `LanguageServerFailed` when the server could not start, or has stopped, or stopped while the request waited, or answered the request with an error of its own. It names the command, so the editor's log says which server failed, and it carries the underlying cause when there is one.

A server that answered with an error is still running, and saying it is not running would send the person looking for a crash that never happened. So the failure carries the server's own words as its `answer`, and the message repeats them. Every other failure has no `answer`, and its message says the server is not running.

=>

export class LanguageServerFailed extends Data.TaggedError('LanguageServerFailed')<{
  readonly command: string
  readonly answer: Option.Option<string>
  readonly cause?: unknown
}> {
  get message(): string {
    return Option.match(this.answer, {
      onSome: (answer) => `the ${this.command} language server answered: ${answer}`,
      onNone: () => `the ${this.command} language server is not running`,
    })
  }
}

## Starting the server

`spawnLanguageServer` starts the server from a command and its arguments, in the folder the server should treat as its workspace, and speaks to it over the process's standard input and output with `vscode-jsonrpc`. It tells the server the language of the files it will open, and what the client can do, as LSP's `initialize` request asks.

The bridge mirrors a running process, so it holds state that the process changes under it: whether the process still runs, and which files it holds open. That state lives in the bridge's own closure and nowhere else, because the process is the one thing outside the program that the program must keep pace with. Everything the bridge hands out, a request's answer or a file's status, is computed from that state at the moment it is asked for.

A process can fail to start, because its command is missing, or cannot be executed, or its folder does not exist. Node reports that failure as an `error` event on the child, after the call that started it has returned. Writing to such a child would fail as well, and an unhandled failure ends the whole language server, which hosts every other language too. So the bridge waits for the child's `spawn` event, which says the process truly started, before it builds a connection or writes a byte. `spawned` is the promise of that event, and it fails with `LanguageServerFailed` on an `error` event instead. A child that started can still close its input later, so the bridge listens for errors on that stream too and takes them as the process stopping. `stopped` runs once, whenever the bridge gives up on the server: when the process exits or fails, when its input breaks, or when the connection reports an error, such as a message it cannot read. It records the moment, disposes the connection, which fails every request still waiting for an answer, and stops the process. A process can outlive a broken connection, and stopping it there keeps a server the bridge can no longer talk to from running on unseen.

=>

export const spawnLanguageServer = (
  command: string,
  args: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  workspace: string,
  languageId: string,
  capabilities: object,
): LanguageServerProcess => {
  const state = { running: true, disposed: false, startedAt: Date.now(), stoppedAt: 0 }
  const child = spawn(command, [...args], { cwd: workspace, stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'ignore'] })
  const stopped = (): void => {
    if (!state.running) return
    state.running = false
    state.stoppedAt = Date.now()
    void connected.then((connection) => connection.dispose()).catch(() => undefined)
    child.kill()
  }
  const spawned = new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {
    child.once('spawn', () => resolve())
    child.once('error', (cause) =>
      reject(new LanguageServerFailed({ command, answer: Option.none(), cause })),
    )
  })
  child.on('exit', stopped)
  child.on('error', stopped)
  child.stdin.on('error', stopped)

~

The bridge imports `vscode-jsonrpc` only when it starts a server. It names the module `vscode-jsonrpc/node.js`, with the extension. The package declares no map of its entry points, so Node resolves a path inside it only as written, and `vscode-jsonrpc/node` names no file to Node. Bun resolves either form, but the VS Code extension and the npm command run the language server on Node. A workspace that never runs a language server never loads the package, and the service itself loads without it, the way it loads without Prettier until it formats. `loaded` is the promise of the module, and `connected` the promise of the connection, built once the process has started and the module has arrived. The connection reports its own failures through `onError` and `onClose`, and the bridge takes either as the server stopping.

A server may receive nothing before it has answered `initialize`. So the bridge keeps `ready`, the promise of that answer, and sends every later message only once `ready` settles. Each message is attached to `ready` in the order the caller sent it, and a promise runs its callbacks in the order they were attached, so the server receives them in that order too. An open document always reaches the server before a request about it.

`connected` and `ready` each get a handler of their own. Every caller attaches to them as well and hears the failure on its own promise, but a promise that fails with no handler at all would be reported as unhandled, and the runtime would end the process for it. The handler on `ready` also calls `stopped`. A server that refused `initialize` but kept running can answer nothing, so the bridge treats it as stopped, and its owner's restart rules apply to it as to a crash.

=>

  const loaded: Promise<Rpc> = spawned.then(() => import('vscode-jsonrpc/node.js'))
  const connected: Promise<MessageConnection> = loaded.then((rpc) => {
    const connection = rpc.createMessageConnection(
      new rpc.StreamMessageReader(child.stdout),
      new rpc.StreamMessageWriter(child.stdin),
    )
    connection.onError(stopped)
    connection.onClose(stopped)
    connection.onRequest(() => null)
    connection.listen()
    return connection
  })

  const ready: Promise<MessageConnection> = connected.then((connection) =>
    connection
      .sendRequest('initialize', {
        processId: process.pid,
        rootUri: URI.file(workspace).toString(),
        workspaceFolders: [{ uri: URI.file(workspace).toString(), name: basename(workspace) }],
        capabilities,
      })
      .then(() => connection.sendNotification('initialized', {}))
      .then(() => connection),
  )
  connected.catch(() => undefined)
  ready.catch(() => stopped())

~

`keepOpen` compares the set it is given with `opened`, the text and version of every file the server holds open, and sorts the difference three ways. `closing` holds the files the server holds but the set leaves out, and each is closed with `didClose`. `opening` holds the files new to the server, and each is sent with `didOpen` at version one. `changing` holds the files whose text differs from what the server holds, and each is sent whole with `didChange`, under the next version. `opened` changes at once, while the messages wait for `ready`, so a second call before the server answers compares with what the first call sent.

=>

  const opened = new Map<string, { readonly text: string; readonly version: number }>()
  const uriOf = (path: string): string => URI.file(path).toString()
  const send = (method: string, params: unknown): void =>
    void ready.then((connection) => connection.sendNotification(method, params)).catch(() => undefined)

  const keepOpen = (files: ReadonlyArray<OpenFile>): void => {
    const wanted = new Set(Array.map(files, (file) => file.path))
    const closing = Array.filter(Array.fromIterable(opened.keys()), (path) => !wanted.has(path))
    const opening = Array.filter(files, (file) => !opened.has(file.path))
    const changing = Array.filter(files, (file) =>
      Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.get(file.path)), {
        onNone: () => false,
        onSome: (held) => held.text !== file.text,
      }),
    )
    closing.forEach((path) => {
      opened.delete(path)
      send('textDocument/didClose', { textDocument: { uri: uriOf(path) } })
    })
    opening.forEach((file) => {
      opened.set(file.path, { text: file.text, version: 1 })
      send('textDocument/didOpen', {
        textDocument: { uri: uriOf(file.path), languageId, version: 1, text: file.text },
      })
    })
    changing.forEach((file) => {
      const version = (opened.get(file.path)?.version ?? 0) + 1
      opened.set(file.path, { text: file.text, version })
      send('textDocument/didChange', {
        textDocument: { uri: uriOf(file.path), version },
        contentChanges: [{ text: file.text }],
      })
    })
  }

~

`request` waits for `ready` like every other message, then sends the request. A server that is no longer running rejects the request at once. A request whose `ready` fails, because the server never started or never answered `initialize`, rejects with `LanguageServerFailed` carrying the cause and no answer, since the server never heard the request.

A request that fails after it was sent needs one more look, because `vscode-jsonrpc` reports two different events the same way. A server's error answer arrives as a `ResponseError` with the code the server sent. But `vscode-jsonrpc` also fails a request with a `ResponseError` of its own when the trouble is on the client's side, under codes it reserves for itself: a message it could not write, a message it could not read, a connection that is not active, and a request still waiting when the connection was disposed. That last one is what a server that crashes mid-request leaves behind. `answerOf` takes a `ResponseError` as the server's answer only when its code is none of `clientCodes`, those four. Anything else, a `ResponseError` under one of them or a closed connection's `ConnectionError`, means the server stopped, and the failure carries no answer.

=>

  const clientCodes = (rpc: Rpc): ReadonlySet<number> =>
    new Set([
      rpc.ErrorCodes.MessageWriteError,
      rpc.ErrorCodes.MessageReadError,
      rpc.ErrorCodes.ConnectionInactive,
      rpc.ErrorCodes.PendingResponseRejected,
    ])

  const answerOf = (rpc: Rpc, cause: unknown): Option.Option<string> =>
    cause instanceof rpc.ResponseError && !clientCodes(rpc).has(cause.code)
      ? Option.some(cause.message)
      : Option.none()

  const asked = <A>(connection: MessageConnection, method: string, params: unknown): Promise<A> =>
    Promise.resolve()
      .then(() => connection.sendRequest<A>(method, params))
      .catch((cause) =>
        loaded.then((rpc) =>
          Promise.reject(new LanguageServerFailed({ command, answer: answerOf(rpc, cause), cause })),
        ),
      )

~

`dispose` runs once. A second call does nothing, so a server shared by several owners is shut down by the first. It asks the server to shut down, as LSP expects, then stops the process. A server that hangs would never answer the shutdown request, so the bridge waits for it two seconds at most, then stops the process regardless.

=>

  const shutdownWait = 2000
  const elapsed = (ms: number): Promise<void> => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms))

  return {
    keepOpen,
    request: <A>(method: string, params: unknown): Promise<A> =>
      state.running
        ? ready.then(
            (connection) => asked<A>(connection, method, params),
            (cause) =>
              Promise.reject(new LanguageServerFailed({ command, answer: Option.none(), cause })),
          )
        : Promise.reject(new LanguageServerFailed({ command, answer: Option.none() })),
    alive: () => state.running,
    uptime: () => (state.running ? Date.now() : state.stoppedAt) - state.startedAt,
    dispose: () => {
      if (state.disposed) return
      state.disposed = true
      const shutdown = ready
        .then((connection) =>
          connection.sendRequest('shutdown').then(() => connection.sendNotification('exit')),
        )
        .catch(() => undefined)
      void Promise.race([shutdown, elapsed(shutdownWait)]).finally(() => {
        stopped()
        child.kill()
      })
    },
  }
}

## What the bridge draws on

Node's `child_process` starts the server, and `path` names its workspace folder. `vscode-jsonrpc` frames the messages on the process's standard streams and pairs each answer with its request. It is a dependency the service imports at run time rather than bundles, for the reason the chapter on bundling the service gives. Only its types are named at the top, the `MessageConnection` and `Rpc`, the shape of the whole module, since the module itself arrives only when a server starts. `URI` turns a path into the `file:` address the protocol names a document by. Effect's `Data` builds the tagged `LanguageServerFailed`, and `Array` and `Option` sort the files the caller wants open.

=>

import { Array, Data, Option } from 'effect'
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process'
import { basename } from 'node:path'
import type { MessageConnection } from 'vscode-jsonrpc/node.js'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'

type Rpc = typeof import('vscode-jsonrpc/node.js')

